{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"2498","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-backlog-core-decompose.md@sha256:3ab456d982025d106a6e8e9fab62b266b12f7c62fb999b8720b20b1301362f10"},"owner_reference":"2498","content":"# Feature Context \u2014 Decompose `backlog_core` operations.py and server.py\n\nItem: #2498 \u00b7 Phase 1 (discovery, WHAT/WHY) \u00b7 Produced 2026-09-03 \u00b7 \u00a78.1 updated 2026-09-03\n\nThis document states the problem, the outcome to achieve, who is affected, what\nconstrains the work, and what must be decided before design. It deliberately does\nnot name target modules or assign functions to them \u2014 that is the architecture\nphase's output.\n\n## 1. Summary\n\n`plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/` is the package behind the backlog MCP\nserver. Two of its modules have grown to a size where they are the dominant\nmaintenance cost in the plugin: `operations.py` (5407 lines) and `server.py`\n(4640 lines) together account for roughly 35% of the package's 28,784 lines\nacross 39 modules. The request is to decompose them into cohesive modules of a\nsize a reader and an agent can hold at once, with no change in behaviour.\n\nThe item has already been groomed with a phased extraction proposal and a\nverification pass. This context document exists to state why the work is worth\ndoing, what \"done\" means in outcome terms, and to surface an architectural\nquestion the original grooming did not account for (Section 8.1, resolved below).\n\n## 2. Problem space\n\n### 2.1 What is actually wrong\n\nVerified against source on 2026-09-03:\n\n| Module | Lines | Top-level defs/classes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `backlog_core/operations.py` | 5407 | 148 |\n| `backlog_core/server.py` | 4640 | ~120 (incl. ~45 `@mcp.tool` handlers) |\n\nThese are not merely long. Three specific properties make them expensive:\n\n- **Mixed altitude within one file.** `operations.py` holds, side by side:\n  GraphQL request plumbing, GitHub issue lifecycle, YAML section normalisation,\n  body slicing and pagination, view assembly, SAM task adapters, milestone and\n  project management, and a union-find implementation for impact-radius conflict\n  analysis. These have no shared invariant beyond \"the backlog touches them\".\n- **Frontend and logic are not separated in `server.py`.** Alongside the\n  `@mcp.tool` handlers sit regex match collection, match-context enrichment,\n  pagination, token counting, section filtering, and over-budget response\n  construction \u2014 logic with no MCP dependency that is only reachable through an\n  MCP tool call.\n- **A concrete defect has already been traced to this shape.** The item records a\n  dual-output anti-pattern in the body section filter: it mutates the result\n  object *and* returns the narrowed body. That is precisely the kind of\n  ambiguous contract that survives in a 5000-line file because no module boundary\n  ever forces the question \"what does this function own?\"\n\n### 2.2 Why it keeps not getting fixed\n\nThe item states that prior attempts stalled on a recurring \"is this a script or a\npackage?\" confusion. That framing itself was corrected on 2026-09-03 (see \u00a78.1):\n`backlog_core/` is not a distributable package and is not being made into one \u2014\nit is the supporting source tree of one PEP 723 entrypoint script, split into\nmultiple files. Treat that as settled context, not an open question.\n\n### 2.3 The cost is paid by agents, not only humans\n\nEvery module in this plugin exists to be consumed by an AI agent harness. An\nagent asked to change one behaviour in `operations.py` must either read 5407\nlines or guess which region matters. That is a direct, recurring context cost on\nevery task touching the backlog, and it compounds: the larger the file, the more\nlikely a change lands in the wrong place, and the more likely the next agent\ninherits a file that is larger still. The file sizes in this package are\nincreasing, not stable \u2014 the item's own figures have drifted upward across three\nseparate grooming passes (5255 \u2192 5407 for `operations.py`; 4684 \u2192 4640 for\n`server.py`).\n\n## 3. Why now\n\n- A defect (#2495) and its fix (#2501, merged) already surfaced the exact seam\n  where the view path is tangled. The investigation cost has been paid; the\n  knowledge is fresh and will decay.\n- #2501 is merged, so the blocking dependency is clear.\n- The package is under active development (recent commits touching\n  `backlog_core` and `dh_core` in the last several merges). Decomposing while\n  the code is being changed anyway is cheaper than decomposing a frozen module\n  later \u2014 but it also means the work has a shelf life before conflicting churn\n  accumulates.\n\n## 4. Desired outcome\n\nStated as observable properties, not as a design:\n\n1. A reader or agent can locate the code responsible for a given backlog\n   behaviour by module name, without reading an unrelated 4000 lines.\n2. Every public symbol that consumers import today remains importable from the\n   same path. No consumer \u2014 test, script, MCP frontend, or sibling package \u2014 is\n   edited to accommodate the move.\n3. The backlog MCP server starts and lists its tools exactly as before.\n4. The full test suite passes with no test modified to accommodate the new\n   structure. A test that must change is a signal that behaviour changed.\n5. The package-vs-launcher decision is recorded in a durable location, so the\n   next session inherits the constraint instead of rediscovering it.\n6. The dual-output contract identified in the view path is resolved to a single\n   unambiguous contract as part of the move, rather than carried forward.\n\nDeliberately *not* an outcome: a uniform line count per module. The target is\ncohesion \u2014 one clear responsibility per module. Line count is a symptom that\nprompted the work, not the acceptance test.\n\n## 5. Non-goals\n\n- No behaviour change, no API change, no new capability.\n- No new distribution boundary: no `pyproject.toml` beside the modules, no new\n  installable package.\n- No conversion of the PEP 723 launcher's style onto the library.\n- No collapse into a single file.\n- No decomposition of the other oversized modules in this package\n  (`gh_client.py` 2098, `models.py` 1937, `parsing.py` 1482,\n  `artifact_provider.py` 1433, `tool_responses.py` 1178, and eight more above\n  700 lines) \u2014 out of scope here, but see Section 8.3.\n\n## 6. Stakeholders and consumers\n\n| Stakeholder | Stake | How they are affected |\n|---|---|---|\n| Backlog MCP server (`backlog_core/server.py`) | Primary frontend | Imports operations through the unified layer; must keep loading and serving all tools |\n| `dh_core.operations` | Unified operations facade | Re-exports `backlog_core.operations` public functions by explicit name list; the names it imports are a hard contract |\n| `sam_schema` CLI and MCP server | Sibling frontends | Share the `dh_core` facade; regressions there are silent until runtime |\n| `agent_profile/server.py` | Separate MCP server | Imports from `backlog_core` |\n| Plugin test suites | Regression safety net | 60 test files across `tests/`, `tests_backlog/`, and `backlog_core/tests/` reference these operations; the item records 617 collected tests in `backlog_core/tests/` alone |\n| Every agent running a dh workflow | Indirect | Any behavioural regression surfaces as a broken grooming, planning, or dispatch workflow, often without an obvious link back to this refactor |\n| Future maintainers/agents | Beneficiary | The whole point: a navigable package |\n\nThe safety net is unusually strong here (617 tests in the package's own suite,\n60 files touching these operations). That is the main reason this refactor is\ntractable at all, and it should be treated as the primary verification asset.\n\n## 7. Risks\n\nOrdered by expected cost.\n\n### R1 \u2014 Silent breakage of the re-export contract (high likelihood, high cost)\n\n`dh_core/operations.py` imports public functions from `backlog_core.operations`\nby an explicit name list, not a wildcard. Any symbol that stops being importable\nfrom its current path breaks the unified facade \u2014 and therefore both frontends \u2014\nat import time. Import-time failure is the *good* case; the bad case is a symbol\nthat resolves to a stale duplicate.\n\nMitigation direction: the set of names importable from each touched module must\nbe treated as a pinned contract with an explicit check, not an assumed\nconsequence of correct editing.\n\n### R2 \u2014 Test suite passes while behaviour changed (medium likelihood, high cost)\n\nPassing pre-existing tests proves no *detected* regression, not correctness. The\nview path in particular has interactions (pagination \u00d7 section filter \u00d7\nover-budget gate) whose combinations may not all be covered. A move that\npreserves each function individually can still change behaviour if a mutation\nside effect is dropped or an import-order dependency is lost.\n\nMitigation direction: capture the observable behaviour of the view path before\nthe move as an explicit baseline, and compare after \u2014 do not rely solely on\n\"the suite is green\".\n\n### R3 \u2014 Fixing the dual-output contract mid-move (medium likelihood, medium cost)\n\nThe item correctly identifies the extraction as the natural fix point for the\nmutate-and-return anti-pattern. But that is a behaviour-affecting change bundled\ninto a \"no behaviour change\" refactor. Its caller count is already known to have\ndrifted (three call sites originally recorded, one found on re-verification),\nwhich means the change's blast radius is not stable knowledge.\n\nMitigation direction: treat the contract fix as separable from the move, so a\nfailure in one is not attributed to the other.\n\n### R4 \u2014 Churn conflict (medium likelihood, medium cost)\n\nThe package is actively changed. A large multi-file move has a short window\nbefore it conflicts with concurrent work. The item's own line-count figures\ndrifting across grooming passes is direct evidence of ongoing churn.\n\n### R5 \u2014 Cosmetic decomposition (medium likelihood, medium cost)\n\nSplitting by line count rather than responsibility produces modules that import\neach other heavily and are no easier to reason about \u2014 while paying the full\nmigration risk. The recorded finding that the candidate seams have a\none-directional dependency (later phases call earlier ones, never the reverse) is\na good sign, but one-directional is a floor, not proof of cohesion.\n\n### R6 \u2014 The stated justification is weaker than it reads (low cost, but affects scope)\n\nSee Section 8.3.\n\n## 8. Open questions\n\nThese are ordered by how much the answer changes the design. 8.1 is resolved\nbelow; 8.2-8.5 remain open for the architecture/planning phases.\n\n### 8.1 Which decomposition target wins \u2014 `backlog_core` siblings, or `dh_core`? \u2014 RESOLVED 2026-09-03\n\n**Original question**: The item prescribes extracting logic \u2014 including logic\ncurrently in `server.py` \u2014 into sibling modules inside `backlog_core/`. The\nrepository also contains a second, active, tracked decomposition effort with a\ndifferent stated destination. `dh_core/__init__.py` states: \"Both the CLI\n(`sam_schema/cli.py`) and MCP servers (`sam_schema/server.py`,\n`backlog_core/server.py`) import from this module. No business logic lives in\nthe frontends.\" `dh_core/operations.py` states it \"is built incrementally... The\ngoal is to reach zero such calls.\" `backlog_core/server.py` already imports\n`from dh_core import operations`, and `tests/test_server_operation_boundary.py`\npins `server.operations is dh_core.operations` as a regression guard. The\nquestion was whether this item's `server.py` extraction target\n(`view_budget.py`) conflicts with that direction.\n\n**Resolution**: No conflict. `grep -n 'def ' dh_core/operations.py | grep -iE\n'view|paginat|budget|section|assembl'` returns zero matches \u2014 none of this\nitem's five `view_budget.py` targets (`_build_over_budget_view`,\n`_build_compact_manifest`, `_filter_view_sections`, `_token_count`,\n`_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET`) exist in `dh_core.operations`. The `server.operations is\ndh_core.operations` regression guard covers a different set of functions\n(`list_labels`, `dispatch_stale_check`, `dispatch_conflicts`) \u2014 genuine\nCRUD/dispatch operations already migrated to the unified layer.\n`backlog_core/operations.py` has zero imports from `dh_core` (one-directional:\n`dh_core` builds on `backlog_core`, not the reverse), so the Phase 1/2 targets\nare equally unaffected.\n\nThe user confirmed the intended direction directly: \"dh_core is the unified\nshared module that sam and backlog both can use, because there's no real\nreason that there should be two separate mcp servers for this one plugin, we\nare converging them over time until one of them goes.\"\n\nThe Design Intent Alignment section's MISSION_ALIGNED verdict \u2014 reached without\nreference to `dh_core` \u2014 is confirmed correct by this resolution, not merely\nassumed.\n\n### 8.2 Should `operations.py` and `server.py` be one piece of work or two? \u2014 RESOLVED 2026-09-03\n\nMoot following 8.1's resolution: the two files no longer have materially\ndifferent risk profiles from a `dh_core` standpoint, since neither's extraction\ntarget conflicts with the `dh_core` migration. No split needed.\n\n### 8.3 What is the actual size guidance, and does it belong in the acceptance criteria?\n\nThe item and its Design Intent Alignment section both cite \"the repository's\n~500-LOC module guidance\", and an acceptance criterion requires each module to\nland \"within (or close to)\" it. That guidance could not be located in\n`AGENTS.md`, `.claude/CLAUDE.md`, `plugins/development-harness/AGENTS.md`, or\n`pyproject.toml`. It appears in `.claude/smells/*.md` code-review artifacts\n(which attribute it to \"code review policy\") and in `.claude/plan/*` refactor\nplans where it applies to *markdown skill files*, not Python modules.\n\nThis matters for two reasons:\n\n- If the threshold is real repo policy, it is undocumented and thirteen further\n  modules in this one package violate it \u2014 so this item is a sample of a\n  package-wide condition, not an isolated cleanup, and the scope boundary in\n  Section 5 deserves a conscious decision rather than an implicit one.\n- If it is a code-review heuristic rather than policy, an acceptance criterion\n  that gates on a line count is gating on the wrong thing. Cohesion is the\n  outcome (Section 4); line count is the symptom that prompted the work.\n\n**Decision needed:** confirm the provenance of the threshold, then either codify\nit or replace the line-count acceptance criterion with a cohesion-based one.\n\n### 8.4 What is the behavioural baseline, and who captures it?\n\nR2 turns on having a pre-move record of the view path's observable output across\nthe pagination / section-filter / over-budget combinations. The item's acceptance\ncriteria say \"the full test suite passes unchanged\" but do not name a baseline\nartifact. Whether the T0 baseline capture is sufficient here, or whether the view\npath needs a purpose-built characterisation pass first, is unresolved.\n\n### 8.5 Is the dual-output contract fix in scope?\n\nSection R3. The item folds it into the extraction. Confirm that is intended, and\nthat it is acceptable for a \"no behavioural change\" refactor to carry one\ndeliberate behavioural change.\n\n## 9. Evidence log\n\nAll figures verified against working-tree source on 2026-09-03.\n\n| Claim | How verified |\n|---|---|\n| `operations.py` 5407 lines, `server.py` 4640 lines; package 28,784 lines / 39 modules | `wc -l backlog_core/*.py` |\n| `operations.py` has 148 top-level defs/classes | `grep -n '^\\(def \\|class \\|async def \\)' operations.py` |\n| `server.py` mixes ~45 `@mcp.tool` handlers with non-MCP logic | `grep -n '^\\(def \\|class \\|async def \\|@mcp\\)' server.py` |\n| `dh_core` is tracked and actively maintained | `git ls-files dh_core/`; `git log -- dh_core/` shows commits through the current HEAD |\n| `dh_core.operations` re-exports `backlog_core.operations` public functions by explicit name | `dh_core/operations.py` line 1243 comment and line 1245 import list |\n| `backlog_core/server.py` already imports `from dh_core import operations` | `server.py` import block |\n| The `server.operations is dh_core.operations` invariant is pinned by a test | `tests/test_server_operation_boundary.py` |\n| \u00a78.1 resolution: no `view/paginat/budget/section/assembl` functions in `dh_core.operations` | `grep -n 'def ' dh_core/operations.py \\| grep -iE 'view\\|paginat\\|budget\\|section\\|assembl'` \u2014 zero matches |\n| \u00a78.1 resolution: `backlog_core/operations.py` has zero `dh_core` imports | `grep -n 'dh_core' backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 zero matches |\n| 60 test files reference these operations | file-list count across `tests/`, `tests_backlog/` |\n| `~500-LOC guidance` not present in AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md / pyproject.toml | repo-wide grep; hits only in `.claude/smells/*` and `.claude/plan/*` |\n| Thirteen further modules in `backlog_core` exceed 500 lines | `wc -l` table above |\n| `dh_core/operations.py` docstring points at `.hermes/plans/unified-backend-progress.md`, which does not exist | `ls .hermes/plans/` \u2014 contains only `evidence/` |\n"}